📁 System partitions (with examples)
/ (root): Core OS files.
Example: Linux system files needed to run your server.

/usr: Installed applications and utilities.
Example: vim, python, or system libraries.

/boot: Boot files.
Example: Linux kernel and GRUB bootloader files.

/opt: Optional or third-party software.
Example: Jenkins installed here (/opt/jenkins workspace).

/home: User personal files and configs.
Example: User xyz’s documents and settings.

/var: Variable data like logs, mail, and databases.
Example: Web server caches or email spool.

/tmp: Temporary files, cleared on reboot.
Example: Temporary upload files during a session.

📊 Logs and monitoring
/var/log: System and application logs.
Example: Apache or system error logs.

/var/log/audit: Security audit logs.
Example: Tracking user login attempts.

/logs: Application-specific logs.
Example: Logs from your custom Java app.

🛠 Project/application data
/Dev1: Development environment or project files.
Example: Source code and configs for Project Dev1.

/data: Main data storage for apps.
Example: User-uploaded files or database storage.

/base: Base templates or static files.
Example: Shared assets for multiple projects.

/keys: Secure keys and certificates.
Example: SSH private keys or SSL certificates.

🩺 Diagnostics
/kdump: Kernel crash dumps for debugging.
Example: Memory snapshot after system crash.

💾 Temporary and runtime memory
tmpfs / devtmpfs: Fast, in-memory storage for temporary system data.
Example: Runtime sockets, shared memory for processes.



